{"id":"dpo-gradient-step","t":"DPO on a bigram language model: sequence log-probs, loss and one gradient step","tags":["Reinforcement Learning","Language Models"],"d":"Hard","rt":"numpy","fn":"dpo_step","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Direct Preference Optimisation trains the policy straight from preference pairs, with no reward model and no sampling. Implement one full step for a tiny bigram language model, including the gradient through the sequence log-probabilities.\n\nWrite `dpo_step(W, W_ref, pairs, beta, lr)` returning `(loss, W_new, chosen_rewards, rejected_rewards)`.\n\n## Model\n$p(y_t\\,|\\,y_{t-1}) = \\mathrm{softmax}(W[y_{t-1}])[y_t]$ with `W` `(V, V)`. A sequence is a list of token ids whose first token is the prompt; its log-probability is $\\log\\pi(y) = \\sum_{t\\ge1}\\log p(y_t|y_{t-1})$ over the response tokens.\n\n## Loss\n`pairs` is a list of `(chosen, rejected)` sequences. With implicit rewards $\\hat r(y) = \\beta\\,(\\log\\pi_W(y) - \\log\\pi_{W_{ref}}(y))$:\n$$\\mathcal L = -\\frac1N\\sum_{n}\\log\\sigma\\big(\\hat r(y_w^{(n)}) - \\hat r(y_l^{(n)})\\big)$$\n\n## Step\nReturn the loss (before the update), $W - \\text{lr}\\,\\partial\\mathcal L/\\partial W$ (`W_ref` is frozen), and the implicit rewards of the chosen and rejected sequences `(N,)` under the **old** `W`.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef dpo_step(W, W_ref, pairs, beta, lr):\n    \"\"\"Return (loss, W_new, chosen_rewards, rejected_rewards).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Take a row-wise log-softmax of $W$ once; a sequence's log-probability is the sum of entries `L[y[t-1], y[t]]`.","With margin $m = \\hat r_w - \\hat r_l$, $\\partial\\mathcal L/\\partial m = -(1 - \\sigma(m))/N$, and $\\partial m/\\partial\\log\\pi(y_w) = \\beta$, $\\partial m/\\partial\\log\\pi(y_l) = -\\beta$.","Each transition $a\\to b$ contributes $\\partial\\log p(b|a)/\\partial W[a] = \\mathbf 1_b - \\mathrm{softmax}(W[a])$; only row $a$ changes."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef _logsm(M):\n    z = M - M.max(-1, keepdims=True)\n    return z - np.log(np.exp(z).sum(-1, keepdims=True))\n\ndef _seq(L, y):\n    y = np.asarray(y)\n    return L[y[:-1], y[1:]].sum()\n\ndef dpo_step(W, W_ref, pairs, beta, lr):\n    W, Wr = np.asarray(W, dtype=float), np.asarray(W_ref, dtype=float)\n    L, Lr = _logsm(W), _logsm(Wr)\n    P = np.exp(L)\n    rc = np.array([beta * (_seq(L, c) - _seq(Lr, c)) for c, _ in pairs])\n    rr = np.array([beta * (_seq(L, r) - _seq(Lr, r)) for _, r in pairs])\n    m = rc - rr\n    loss = float(np.logaddexp(0, -m).mean())\n    coef = -beta * (1 - 1 / (1 + np.exp(-m))) / len(m)\n    G = np.zeros_like(W)\n    for k, (c, r) in enumerate(pairs):\n        for y, sgn in ((c, 1.0), (r, -1.0)):\n            y = np.asarray(y)\n            for a, b in zip(y[:-1], y[1:]):\n                G[a] -= sgn * coef[k] * P[a]\n                G[a, b] += sgn * coef[k]\n    return loss, W - lr * G, rc, rr\n","ex_md":"## The derivation in one line\nThe KL-regularised RLHF objective has the closed-form optimum $\\pi^*(y|x) \\propto \\pi_{ref}(y|x)\\,e^{r(x,y)/\\beta}$. Solving for $r$ gives $r = \\beta\\log(\\pi^*/\\pi_{ref}) + \\beta\\log Z(x)$, and in the Bradley-Terry likelihood of a preference pair the intractable $\\log Z(x)$ cancels. So the reward model can be replaced by the policy's own log-ratio, and preference learning becomes a classification loss on the policy.\n\n## What the gradient does\nEach pair's gradient is weighted by $1 - \\sigma(m)$, how wrong the implicit reward currently ranks the pair, and pushes up the chosen sequence's log-probability and down the rejected one's, token by token. When the policy equals the reference (tests 1 and 5) every margin is 0 and the loss is $\\log 2$.\n\n## Practical notes\n- Both log-probabilities are summed over the response only; the prompt is conditioning.\n- The policy can lower both chosen and rejected likelihoods while still widening the margin, which is a known failure mode; variants add an SFT term on the chosen responses.\n- Longer responses get larger-magnitude log-probabilities, which can bias DPO towards length; some variants normalise by length.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Forgetting to subtract the reference log-probabilities.\n- Flipping the sign of the rejected term in the gradient.\n- Updating the reference model.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Why is DPO \"offline\", and what do online or iterative variants change?\n- What does $\\beta$ control, and what happens as it goes to 0?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[-0.54380831, -0.37440287, -1.42763813],\n       [-0.66018291,  0.85561917,  1.26304858],\n       [ 1.1686392 , -2.1395124 , -0.87787132]])\narray([[-0.54380831, -0.37440287, -1.42763813],\n       [-0.66018291,  0.85561917,  1.26304858],\n       [ 1.1686392 , -2.1395124 , -0.87787132]])\n[([0, 1, 2], [0, 0, 0])]\n0.1\n1.0","out":"(0.6931471805599453, array([[-0.62456123, -0.30160278, -1.41968529],\n       [-0.66421644,  0.83725425,  1.28544703],\n       [ 1.1686392 , -2.1395124 , -0.87787132]]), array([0.]), array([0.]))"},{"in":"array([[ 0.14726499, -1.80224286,  0.65787051, -1.90951871],\n       [-0.27840308,  0.46348543,  1.43101191, -0.23242305],\n       [-0.3566817 ,  3.65661203,  1.27435675, -1.55882408],\n       [ 0.29461446, -1.28427833, -2.47797423,  1.46786363]])\narray([[-0.07509107, -1.34750781,  1.14444472, -1.55728557],\n       [-0.50143873,  1.00474667,  1.26877512, -0.12602284],\n       [-0.30968015,  3.92422197,  1.8834075 , -1.44166937],\n       [ 0.12189121, -1.10050952, -2.17403775,  1.36236114]])\n[([3, 1], [3, 3, 0, 2]), ([1, 3, 2, 0], [1, 1, 3, 0])]\n0.1\n1.0","out":"(0.6909920340733356, array([[ 0.15584918, -1.80102095,  0.64696679, -1.90842109],\n       [-0.27585138,  0.44416194,  1.44511193, -0.22975128],\n       [-0.33239966,  3.6344927 ,  1.27231421, -1.55894424],\n       [ 0.2503253 , -1.257916  , -2.4529424 ,  1.46075863]]), array([-0.02813529, -0.01979486]), array([-0.01153071, -0.04525095]))"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../deep-learning/index.html#l08-07","t":"Learning from preferences","c":"Deep Learning"}],"related":["dpo-closed-form-loss","bradley-terry-reward-model-loss","compare-sequence-and-token-length-normalization"],"ntests":5,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-0.5438083065037068, -0.3744028714900358, -1.4276381263708386], [-0.6601829123128888, 0.8556191739656844, 1.2630485830468214], [1.1686391984078701, -2.1395124047573764, -0.8778713190324648]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[-0.5438083065037068, -0.3744028714900358, -1.4276381263708386], [-0.6601829123128888, 0.8556191739656844, 1.2630485830468214], [1.1686391984078701, -2.1395124047573764, -0.8778713190324648]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, [{\"__tup__\": [[0, 1, 2], [0, 0, 0]]}], 0.1, 1.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [0.6931471805599453, {\"__nd__\": [[-0.6245612344544285, -0.3016027837809506, -1.4196852861292022], [-0.664216436501824, 0.8372542494568322, 1.285447031744609], [1.1686391984078701, -2.1395124047573764, -0.8778713190324648]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.14726499251647995, -1.8022428595256659, 0.6578705052339205, -1.9095187060528989], [-0.27840308006226533, 0.46348543416554777, 1.4310119073388388, -0.2324230516682338], [-0.3566816993372196, 3.65661203413299, 1.2743567514018324, -1.5588240835625746], [0.29461445744939374, -1.2842783328446368, -2.477974233911381, 1.4678636281347928]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[-0.07509107151022903, -1.3475078136920133, 1.1444447207874797, -1.5572855699633699], [-0.5014387274339198, 1.0047466683237471, 1.2687751196484167, -0.126022838555224], [-0.30968015159030954, 3.9242219695765663, 1.8834075007391122, -1.441669365864185], [0.12189121437591255, -1.1005095172415493, -2.1740377481683284, 1.3623611434881182]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, [{\"__tup__\": [[3, 1], [3, 3, 0, 2]]}, {\"__tup__\": [[1, 3, 2, 0], [1, 1, 3, 0]]}], 0.1, 1.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [0.6